@inject ApiMetadata Api
@* Alert's Overview, written by hand after the selection and form field Overviews: status and
   variants, dismissing (previewed in each mode), an alert a post renders, and its own words.
   Accessibility and See also come from the doc comment. *@

<Heading Level="2">Usage</Heading>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Usage</Code>
<TextBlock>An alert is part of the page, next to what it's about: a form's result, a warning above a table. For a message about the whole app, use a <Anchor Href="docs/components/banner">Banner</Anchor>; for news that needs no place on the page, a toast from the <Anchor Href="docs/components/toast-region">ToastRegion</Anchor>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Status</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>Status</Code> for what the message means: it sets the color, the icon and the word screen readers hear before the message. <Code>Icon</Code> puts another icon in its place, and <Code>HideIcon</Code> leaves it out.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="alert-statuses" />

<Heading Level="2">Variants</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>Variant</Code> for the weight: <Code>Soft</Code> (the default), <Code>Outline</Code> or <Code>Solid</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="alert-variants" />

<Heading Level="2">Actions and dismissing</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>Dismissible</Code> for a close button, which works with JavaScript off too, and an <Code>AlertActions</Code> written last in the message for buttons or links under it.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="alert-dismiss" />
<TextBlock>Closing isn't remembered: on a static page the next load or post renders the alert again. For a message that should stay closed on every page, use a Banner, which keeps the choice in a cookie. On an interactive page, <Code>@@bind-Dismissed</Code> tells C# it closed, and setting <Code>Dismissed</Code> back to false shows it again.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">After a post</Heading>
<TextBlock>Render an alert from the handler to say what a post did: it comes with the response, so it shows in every mode, with or without a reload.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="alert-saved" />
<TextBlock>Show what was saved, not the field: on an interactive page the field changes as people type. There, an alert closed once stays closed until <Code>Dismissed</Code> changes, so the example binds it and clears it on each save. On a static page each response draws the alert afresh.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Its own words</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>StatusLabel</Code> for the word read before the message ("Info", "Success", "Warning" or "Error"; nothing for Neutral; <Code>""</Code> leaves it out), and <Code>DismissLabel</Code> for the close button's name ("Dismiss"). <Anchor Href="docs/common/text">Text and other languages</Anchor> sets them for the whole app.</TextBlock>

@if (OverviewParts.Of(Component, Api).Count > 0)
{
    <Heading Level="2">Parts</Heading>
}
<OverviewParts Component="Component" />

<Heading Level="2">Mode support</Heading>
<ModeSupportTable Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@if (ApiComponent.Accessibility is not null)
{
    <Heading Level="2">Accessibility</Heading>
    <DocHtml Html="@ApiComponent.Accessibility" Context="@(Context with { Overview = true })" Size="ProseSize.Md" />
}

<Heading Level="2">Parameters</Heading>
<ParameterTable ApiComponent="ApiComponent" ApiHref="@Registry.ApiPageHref(Component)" Context="Context" Compact />

<OverviewSeeAlso Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public RegistryComponent Component { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiComponent ApiComponent { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiPageContext Context { get; set; } = null!;

    private const string Usage = """
        <Alert Status="Status.Success" Title="Saved">Your changes are live.</Alert>

        @* A close button that works with JavaScript off too. *@
        <Alert Status="Status.Warning" Dismissible>Your trial ends in 3 days.</Alert>

        @* Buttons or links under the message, written last. *@
        <Alert Status="Status.Danger" Title="Couldn't save">
            The server didn't respond.
            <AlertActions><Button Size="ControlSize.Sm">Try again</Button></AlertActions>
        </Alert>
        """;
}
